> Is this still true regarding shape creation as well?

@Zylann Yes, this applies to shape creation in general as well, same as with the extension.

> In my voxel terrain, I regularly create a lot of `ConcavePolygonShape3D` at runtime for each chunk, but it's expensive because it involves BVH calculations. The inability to do this from inside my own meshing threads has limited streaming performance for a long time, since I had to defer it to the main thread. I did this originally because previous physics engines in Godot had no guarantee that this was safe. Now even if this is made safe, I need a way to have control over when the heavy lifting happens (see also [godotengine/godot-proposals#483](https://github.com/godotengine/godot-proposals/issues/483), though a bit old).

@Zylann I'm all for finding some solution to this, but I'm not entirely sure what that would be. Since we're now relying on the same synchronization mechanism as Godot Physics, any `shape_set_data` call will end up queued in the command buffer of `PhysicsServer3DWrapMT` and later executed (in serial) on the server thread anyway, when synchronized with the main thread.

You could make `shape_set_data` a non-deferred method, similar to methods like `concave_polygon_shape_create`, but since the shape could be attached to a body you'll have knock-on effects that now need to be thread-safe as well, which will likely be error-prone to maintain.

> The **Bounce Velocity Threshold** and **Sleep Velocity Threshold** project settings are missing range hints, as they shouldn't be allowed to be negative (or even zero, perhaps):

@Calinou I've allowed zero for now, but I struggle to think of sensible ranges and step sizes for these, so I've put in `0,1,0.001,or_greater`, meaning a minimum of 0, a "maximum" of 1, a step size of 0.001 and no upper bound (when inputting the value manually).

Do you have any suggestions for better ranges for these, @jrouwe, or just a better lower bound?

> The [3D Physics Tests](https://github.com/godotengine/godot-demo-projects/tree/master/3d/physics_tests) demo crashes on startup, likely because of debug collision shape drawing. This doesn't occur on 4.4.dev5.

@Calinou Is there anything in particular I have to do to trigger this, besides just starting the demo? I don't seem to be able to reproduce this (on Windows).

> On my end, I just need to start the demo (even through the command line using `--path path/to/project/folder`). I'm on Linux.

@Calinou I just tried on latest Linux Mint, and it seems to work fine for me there, weirdly enough.

Is anyone else here able to run the [3D physics tests](https://github.com/godotengine/godot-demo-projects/tree/master/3d/physics_tests) project on Linux to see if it crashes?
